    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a solid way to handle the provenance gap. My main concern is the \"feeling\" of the records. When we open these files, there's this sense that the creators aren't just \"anonymous,\" but like... intentionally hidden or even possibly non-human or something just outside our conventional understanding of 19th-century history. I know that sounds a bit unscientific, but is there any precedent in archival science for dealing with \"anomalous\" or \"unclassifiable\" entities that don't fit into standard human-centric taxonomies? I'm trying to figure out if there's a way to record these \"outlier\" origins without sounding like I'm writing fiction.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In archival science, when records defy conventional categorization, the practice is to document the *nature* of the anomaly rather than trying to force it into a known category. You can use specialized fields, perhaps extending Dublin Core or employing descriptive notes in `dc:subject`, to flag the record as containing \"Unconventional Attribution\" or \"Provenance Anomaly.\" It’s crucial to keep the language objective, describing the *metadata problem*—that the creator/origin cannot be mapped to known historical agents—rather than speculating on their nature.\n\nThis approach is a professional way of saying, \"This record is a puzzle, and we are noting that it is a puzzle.\" It allows future researchers to investigate the *why* of the mystery without the metadata itself becoming speculative fiction. You are essentially tagging the record as needing further, deep-level investigation, which is exactly what a good metadata schema should facilitate.",
      "source": "target",
